---
// Privacy Policy — PLACEHOLDER. See terms.astro for why this page exists.
//
// 2026-08-10: the in-person disaster check was removed from the product, so the
// clause promising that a recipient's details would be passed to the Cajun Navy
// Ground Force for a door knock is gone. It is replaced by an explicit denial
// rather than silence — this page previously told people their parent's address
// would be handed to a third party, and anyone who read it deserves to see that
// withdrawn in as many words. The ZIP code's stated purpose is now the true one:
// it sets the recipient's time zone so the call lands at the right hour.
//
// 2026-08-31: the "what we do with call details" clause understated the
// product. It described alerting on concerns, while the dashboard has been
// showing the account holder a written summary of EVERY call — good ones
// included. Silence about routine disclosure is the worse error of the two, so
// it now says plainly what the account holder sees. The audience sentence is
// deliberately narrower than it may read: the "own calls" RLS policy scopes
// SELECT to accounts.auth_user_id = auth.uid(), so emergency contacts, who are
// contacts rows and not auth users, cannot sign in at all. They see a summary
// only inside a concern email.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import LegalDraft from '../components/LegalDraft.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Privacy Policy · Aunt Mabel" noindex={true}>
  <LegalDraft heading="Privacy Policy" kicker="Legal">
    <p>
      This policy will describe how <strong>Trustlight LLC</strong>, the company
      that operates the Aunt Mabel daily wellness-call service, handles the
      information collected through it.
    </p>
    <p class="emphasis">
      We do not share a recipient's details with the Cajun Navy Ground Force.
    </p>
    <p>
      This policy is being finalized with counsel now. When it's ready, this page
      will set out what we collect, why we collect it, how long we keep it, who
      it is shared with, and how to ask for it to be deleted.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two things are worth stating up front, because they're the ones families
      ask about first:
    </p>
    <p class="emphasis">
      After every call, the account holder sees a short summary of how it went —
      how your loved one sounded, what they talked about, and anything that gave
      us concern. This is written by Mabel after the call, not a recording or a
      transcript of what was said. It appears in the account holder's dashboard;
      the emergency contacts named during enrollment cannot sign in and read it
      there. If something sounds wrong we email the account holder and those
      emergency contacts rather than waiting for anyone to check the dashboard,
      and that email includes the summary.
    </p>
    <p>
      A recipient's ZIP code is used to work out their time zone, so the daily
      call arrives at the right hour where they actually are.
    </p>
  </LegalDraft>
</BaseLayout>
